ResNet’s skip connections were He et al.’s solution to vanishing gradients in deep networks. The flexible information management the Longon paper documents is an emergent property of that design choice, not an intentional feature. Nobody built ResNet thinking “we need a skip-overwrite spectrum for information management.” They built it thinking “deep networks won’t train without shortcut connections.”

And your CNN is the standard architecture for this kind of task. Nobody would look at a 3-block CNN for character classification and say “you should add residual connections.” It’s three layers deep. There’s no vanishing gradient problem to solve. The architecture is conventional, appropriate for the task as described, and used everywhere in the literature for comparable problems.

The standard tool for this standard task has exactly one information management mode. Nobody noticed because nobody checked what happens below zero.

